    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Description\nTo secure remote editing, Neovim needs to enforce strict authentication for TCP sockets to prevent unauthorized Remote Code Execution (RCE). \n\nThis issue covers **Phase 1**: Token Generation and Secure Storage.\n\nWhen a user starts a TCP server (e.g., `nvim --headless --listen 127.0.0.1:6666`), Neovim should:\n1. Generate a 32-byte cryptographically secure random token using `os_getrandom()`.\n2. Write this token to a temporary file located in `stdpaths_get_xdg_var(kXDGRuntimeDir)` (e.g., `~/.local/state/nvim/tcp_auth_<pid>`).\n3. Ensure the file is created with strict `0600` permissions (readable/writable only by the owner).\n\n### Sequence Diagram\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant Neovim\n    participant Filesystem\n    \n    User->>Neovim: Start TCP server (--listen)\n    activate Neovim\n    Neovim->>Neovim: os_getrandom() (32 bytes)\n    Neovim->>Filesystem: Write to ~/.local/state/nvim/tcp_auth_<pid>\n    Note right of Filesystem: chmod 0600\n    Neovim-->>User: Server listening\n    deactivate Neovim\n```\n<!-- Attach issue1.gif from CS/scratch/ here -->\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hi team.\n\nI'm opening this issue (along with #40821 and #40822) to propose a structured solution for securing Neovim's headless TCP sockets. Currently, the lack of authentication on TCP sockets leaves remote editing setups vulnerable to arbitrary RPC execution.\n\nI've broken the proposed architecture down into three phases:\n1. **Token Generation & Storage (This issue)** \n2. **Channel State & Enforcement ([#40821](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/40821))**\n3. **Client-Side Handshake ([#40822](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/40822))**\n\nI am planning to implement this myself and would love to get your thoughts or feedback on this architectural approach before I start opening PRs. Let me know if you see any immediate red flags or anything like that, y'all.",
